# Force LF line endings on test-vector JSON files so SHA-256 lock tests
# (e.g. tests/vectors_schema.rs::v0_2_sha256_lock_matches_committed_file)
# match across platforms. Without this rule, Windows checkout converts
# LF → CRLF and the on-disk byte sequence diverges from the locked SHA.
crates/md-codec/tests/vectors/*.json text eol=lf

# Committed `cargo vendor` tree (reproducible-builds-musl cycle, P3b) — store
# EVERY vendored file VERBATIM, byte-for-byte. `cargo vendor` writes a
# `.cargo-checksum.json` per crate carrying the SHA-256 of each file's ORIGINAL
# bytes; the `--locked --offline` reproducible build verifies those checksums.
# git's default text/eol handling would CRLF→LF-normalize the vendored files
# that ship with CRLF (e.g. `vendor/zerocopy/win-cargo.bat`) on commit, mutating
# their bytes out from under the committed checksum → a clean checkout would fail
# the offline build with a checksum mismatch. `-text` disables all EOL
# normalization + binary/text detection for the vendor tree so the committed
# bytes match what `cargo vendor` emitted (and what the checksums pin).
vendor/** -text

# The four-repo shared conformance corpus. Its SHA-256 is pinned here and in
# three sibling repos; `* text=auto` leaves it native-eol, so a Windows
# checkout CRLF-converts it and the byte-level hash drifts. Measured
# 2026-08-27 on mnemonic-key: three windows-latest jobs RED at that exact
# assertion while Linux, macOS, musl and FreeBSD stayed green.
# MUST sort after any `* text=auto` (last-match-wins per attribute).
design/display-grouping-vectors.tsv text eol=lf
